course: history
lesson: 74
title: Cities then and now: the biggest cities of every age and of today
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The title «biggest city in the world» is like a crown passed from hand to hand: Uruk in Mesopotamia, then Rome, then Chang'an in China, Baghdad, Beijing, London from about 1825, New York and then Tokyo; the centre of wealth and power moved, and the crown moved with it.
explain: Today the answer depends on where you draw the city's edge: a 2025 UN report put Jakarta first, Dhaka second and Tokyo third, while older lists put Tokyo on top, so three friends can each be right by a different rule.
explain: Cities of more than 10 million people are called megacities; there are now more than 30, most of them in Asia, Africa and Latin America, and Lagos and Kinshasa are among the fastest growing on Earth.
explain: The past had big cities that outsiders forgot: Cahokia, by the Mississippi, was about as big as London around 1100, and Angkor in Cambodia was never really «lost», since monks and local people kept using its great temple.
explain: In a strategy game a city is a dot with a number; in real life it is millions of people who need homes, water, food from far-away farms and a way to get to work.
fact: The world's biggest city has changed many times: from Mesopotamian cities like Uruk, to Rome, to Chinese cities like Chang'an, to Baghdad, to Beijing, to London from about 1825 to 1925, then to New York and later Tokyo.
fact: Which city is biggest today depends on how its edge is drawn: a 2025 UN report ranked Jakarta first, Dhaka second and Tokyo third, while older lists put Tokyo first.
fact: Cities of more than 10 million people are called megacities, and there are now more than 30 of them.
fact: African cities such as Lagos in Nigeria and Kinshasa in the Democratic Republic of the Congo are among the fastest growing in the world.
fact: About a billion people live in informal settlements, often called slums, mostly because cities grow faster than proper homes, pipes and roads can be built.
fact: The first skyscraper, built in Chicago in 1885, had about 10 floors; the Burj Khalifa in Dubai, opened in 2010, is 828 metres tall.
fact: Tall buildings became possible with steel frames and with safe lifts, shown by Elisha Otis in the 1850s, which stop the lift falling if a cable breaks.
fact: Angkor in Cambodia was never fully lost: monks and local people kept using Angkor Wat, and French visitors in the 1800s made it famous in Europe.
fact: Cahokia, near today's St Louis in the USA, was built by Mississippian people and had perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 people around 1100, about as many as London then; the numbers are estimates.
fact: Some capitals were planned from scratch to be fair between regions: Washington DC in the 1790s, Canberra chosen in 1908 and founded in 1913, and Brasília in 1960.
fact: In the USA only about 2 people in 100 work on farms, but with machines they grow food for many millions of city people.
fact: Many cities are trying car-free centres, more bikes and more trees to cool hot streets; some people like it, others worry about shops and getting around, so it is debated.
exercise: Which of these cities was once the biggest in the world? | Chang'an in China | Reykjavik in Iceland | Oslo in Norway | Canberra in Australia
exercise: Roughly when was London the world's biggest city? | From about 1825 to 1925 | In Roman times | From about 1400 to 1500 | Only since 2020
exercise: Why can people disagree about which city is biggest today? | It depends on where you draw the city's edge | Some cities refuse to let anyone count their people | Nobody has ever counted the people in any city | Cities change size every hour of the day
exercise: Which city did a 2025 UN report put first? | Jakarta | London, in England | Paris, in France | Ancient Rome
exercise: What is a megacity? | A city of more than 10 million people | Any old city that has a castle in it | A city built on water, like Venice | A city made up of only skyscrapers
exercise: Which of these is among the world's fastest growing cities? | Lagos | Venice | Edinburgh | Reykjavik
exercise: Why do many people live in informal settlements, or slums? | Cities grow faster than proper homes can be built | People prefer living without water or electricity | Governments order everyone to live there for a year | Only tourists on holiday live there
exercise: About how many people live in informal settlements? | About a billion | About a hundred | Nobody today | Everyone in every city
exercise: What invention made very tall buildings practical? | Safe lifts that stop if a cable breaks | Very long wooden ladders | Big glass windows that open | Electric doorbells on every floor of the block
exercise: How tall is the Burj Khalifa? | 828 metres | About 82 metres | About 8 kilometres | Only 28 metres
exercise: Why is it wrong to say Angkor was «lost in the jungle until explorers found it»? | Monks and local people kept using Angkor Wat | Angkor was built by the explorers themselves in the 1800s | Angkor is in the middle of a desert, not a jungle | Angkor was never built at all; it is only a legend
exercise: What was Cahokia? | A big city built by Mississippian people around 1100 | A Spanish fort built on the river in the 1700s | A Viking village on the coast of Canada | A city founded by the Romans in America
exercise: Why were capitals like Brasília and Canberra built from scratch? | To be fair between regions of the country | Because the old capitals sank into the sea | To hide the government from tourists | Because a game told them to
exercise: In the USA, about how many people in 100 work on farms? | About 2 | About 50 | About 90 | None at all
exercise: Why are some cities trying car-free centres? | To make streets cleaner, cooler and safer, though it is debated | Because cars have been banned in all the cities of the world since 2020 | Because nobody in a city owns a car anymore | To make the traffic jams longer, so people stay home
test: Tariq: "Tokyo's the biggest city, easy." His cousin: "Nah bhai, Dhaka!" ("Bhai" means brother.) His mate from Jakarta: "No way, Jakarta!" Who's right? | It depends on the counting rules; a 2025 UN report put Jakarta first | Only Tariq: Tokyo is the biggest by every way of counting, easy | Only the cousin; Dhaka is twice as big as Tokyo | None of them; London is still the biggest
test: Mei rattles off: "Uruk, Rome, Chang'an, London, New York, Tokyo!" Her mate: "The biggest city was always in Europe, innit." What's fair? | The crown moved around the world, often in Asia | Her mate is right: the biggest was always in Europe | The biggest city was always Rome, right up to today | Cities were never measured before 2000
test: Jayden: "Megacities? Like, mega-huge, skibidi huge?" What's the real meaning? | Cities of more than 10 million people | Cities guarded by giant robots | Cities with more than 100 people | Cities made of one single giant building
test: Ade from Lagos: "Lagos dey grow sharp sharp, e be like say e no go stop o!" ("Sharp sharp" means very fast, in Nigerian Pidgin.) What does this show? | Some of the fastest growing cities are in Africa | Lagos is getting smaller | Big cities are only in rich countries | Lagos stopped growing in 1900
test: Sofia sees tin-roof homes on telly. Her cousin: "They just like living like that, it's their vibe." Fairer view? | Cities grow faster than proper homes, pipes and roads can be built | People choose them because they love the sound of rain on their tin roofs | They are holiday cabins for tourists | Such homes only exist in films and on telly
test: Ben stares at the Burj Khalifa: "Bro, how is it not falling down?" What would happen if there were no safe lifts? | Very tall buildings would be almost useless for people | Buildings would just grow taller and taller | Stairs would be faster than lifts anyway, so nothing | Nothing at all, bro, lifts are just there for fun
test: Liam's mate: "Angkor was lost in the jungle till explorers found it." Liam: "Sus." ("Sus" means suspicious.) Why is Liam right to doubt it? | Local monks and people kept using Angkor Wat all along | Explorers built Angkor themselves and then said they found it | Angkor is in Europe, not in any jungle | Angkor was found on the Moon by astronauts
test: Ava's Missouri uncle: "Y'all, there wasn't nothin' here but prairie before St Louis." ("Y'all" means you all.) What does Cahokia show? | A big city of maybe 10,000 to 20,000 people stood nearby around 1100 | He is right: no one at all lived there before the French came in the 1700s | Cahokia was built by the French in 1900 | Cahokia was a Roman camp
test: Leo: "Why build a whole capital from scratch, that's mad." What's a reason? | To be fair between regions instead of choosing an old rival city | Because the old capital ran out of bricks and could not grow any more | Because the President liked camping | Because games told them to
test: Kai from an Iowa farm: "City kids think food comes from shops lol." What is true? | A few farmers with machines grow food for millions of city people | Most city people grow all their own food on their balconies and roofs | Shops make the food in the back room | Farms are only found inside cities
test: Emma's dad, a bit salty: "No cars in the centre? How'm I s'posed to get to work?" ("Salty" means annoyed.) What's the balanced view? | Car-free centres have fans and critics, so it is debated | Everyone agrees cars must go, so your dad will just have to walk | Everyone agrees nothing should change | Cars are already banned everywhere
test: In Europa Universalis, Mei's capital Beijing has the biggest number on the map for centuries. What does real history say? | Beijing was indeed among the world's biggest cities for long periods | Beijing was a small village until 2000 | Beijing never existed before 1900, when it was built as a new capital | Cities in games always match history exactly
exam: Identify the city which a report of the United Nations published in two thousand and twenty-five ranked as the most populous. | Jakarta | London, England | Rome, Italy | Canberra, Australia
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the designation «megacity»? | An urban area having a population in excess of ten million persons | Any urban area possessing a castle or a cathedral of great antiquity | An urban area constructed upon water | An urban area consisting solely of skyscrapers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Cahokia. | It was a substantial settlement of Mississippian people, of perhaps ten to twenty thousand inhabitants around eleven hundred | It was a Roman encampment of the first century | It was established by French settlers in the twentieth century | It was a temple complex in Cambodia
exam: Ascertain the reason for which certain capitals, such as Brasília and Canberra, were established anew. | To be impartial as between the regions of the state | Because the former capitals had been submerged by the sea | To conceal the government from foreign visitors | Because no other cities existed in those states
